      ":question/id": "fe198673-508e-5cdd-80a3-449f65da96c3",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\\frac{dy}{dt}= \\\\ \\left(y - 1\\right)^{2} \\ln{\\left(y - 1 \\right)} \\\\ + e^{-2}\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following are equilibrium solutions? Select all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "An equilibrium is a constant function $y(t)=c$. Set $dy/dt=0$ and test the resulting equation.\n\nFirst keep the original domain: $y - 1>0$.\n\nTest whether $f(c)=0$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}f(c)= \\\\ \\left(c - 1\\right)^{2} \\ln{\\left(c - 1 \\right)} \\\\ + e^{-2}\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\n- For $c=1 + e$: Use $\\ln{\\left(e \\right)}=1$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}f(1 + e)= \\\\ e^{-2} + e^{2} \\ln{\\left(e \\right)} \\\\ =e^{-2} + e^{2}\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nThis is nonzero, so it is not an equilibrium solution.\n\n- For $c=2$: Use $\\ln{\\left(1 \\right)}=0$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) &=1 \\ln{\\left(1 \\right)} + e^{-2} \\\\ &=e^{-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is nonzero, so it is not an equilibrium solution.\n\n- For $c=e^{-1} + 1$: Use $\\ln{\\left(e^{-1} \\right)}=-1$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}f(e^{-1} + 1)= \\\\ \\frac{\\ln{\\left(e^{-1} \\right)}}{e^{2}} + e^{-2} \\\\ =0\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nThis is zero, so it is an equilibrium solution.\n\nSelect $y=e^{-1} + 1$."
    },
